{"title":"2001 AIME I Problems/Problem 3","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Find the sum of the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Root\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Root\">roots</a>, real and non-real, of the equation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/b/bcb74a6364aff97904998478796a3272efa03104.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^{2001}+\\left(\\frac 12-x\\right)^{2001}=0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"192\" height=\"46\" />, given that there are no multiple roots.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>By <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Vieta%27s_formulas\" title=\"Vieta&#39;s formulas\">Vieta's formulae</a>, for a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Polynomial\" title=\"Polynomial\">polynomial</a> equation of the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/9/ab9a1f4b05a3ae2a802d997b7b53030b4c3191ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_nx^n + a_{n-1}x^{n-1} + \\dotsb + a_0 = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"250\" height=\"21\" />, the sum of the roots, <i>counted with multiplicity</i>, is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/8/11844fa0924f69696891ffb0e6a9d43445d60774.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-\\frac{a_{n-1}}{a_n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"56\" height=\"37\" />. \n</p><p>Now, by the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Binomial_Theorem\" title=\"Binomial Theorem\">Binomial Theorem</a>, the highest-degree term of the expansion of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/d/c/9dcd966ef99cf22ce640f3406036c19eb733cd1e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(\\frac 12-x\\right)^{2001}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"97\" height=\"46\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/c/afcef6b6a14b8484d1d1730da25b25c5f79f0491.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-x^{2001}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"54\" height=\"18\" />, but <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/e/8fe61f1e8ab5e63901149156f2e8851210ab4ea1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^{2001}+\\left(-x^{2001}\\right) = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -7px\" width=\"163\" height=\"23\" />, so the highest-degree term is in fact the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/6/b1653833651a71a5912a387441582cbc76283a80.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^{2000}$\" width=\"36\" height=\"15\" /> term. Since there are no multiple roots, counting them with multiplicity includes each root exactly once, so the required sum will be given by precisely the formula above.\n</p><p>We accordingly need to compute the coefficients of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/6/b1653833651a71a5912a387441582cbc76283a80.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^{2000}$\" width=\"36\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/a/42ab66a6ee352c5464a64ce1faaf8e7bee4f52a6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^{1999}$\" width=\"36\" height=\"15\" /> terms, which is straightforward using the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Binomial_Theorem\" title=\"Binomial Theorem\">Binomial Theorem</a> again:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/9/7295dcd2f83bad418cff02c1c72b15c3436d357f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*}&amp;\\binom{2001}{1} \\cdot (-x)^{2000} \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^1 =\\frac{2001}{2}x^{2000}, \\quad\\text{and} \\\\ &amp;\\binom{2001}{2} \\cdot (-x)^{1999} \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^2 =-\\frac{2001 \\cdot 2000}{8}x^{1999} = -2001 \\cdot 250x^{1999}. \\end{align*}\" width=\"521\" height=\"113\" />\n</p><p>Hence the sum of all the distinct roots is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/0/cf05c92617f9ab454960a8d624381ba6d5b42621.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-\\frac{-2001 \\cdot 250}{\\left(\\frac{2001}{2}\\right)} = 250 \\cdot 2 = \\boxed{500}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"247\" height=\"45\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>As in Solution 1, we find that the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/4/0/c40d770ced37c820b7a925a793e013a71d9cc7fe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^{2001}$\" width=\"35\" height=\"15\" /> terms cancel, so the left-hand side of the given equation is a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/8/a986ec38b67983e544439ec2852ca7da0e44fe25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2000^{\\text{th}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"52\" height=\"17\" />-degree polynomial. This means there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/f/05f1ffc8a6829f25b0b0366087a70dfd4e06fef6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2000$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" /> roots, <i>counted with multiplicity</i>, but as there are no multiple roots, all of these <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/f/05f1ffc8a6829f25b0b0366087a70dfd4e06fef6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2000$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" /> roots are actually distinct. Moreover, noting the symmetry of the equation in the form given, we observe that if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> is a root, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/a/3/2a3dde49be47892e04bbe1947027099acf43a862.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(\\frac{1}{2}-x\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"74\" height=\"45\" /> is also a root. In each case, we must have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/6/65654cb30ea56e099dfb358e3167a295a7c58bc2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\neq \\frac{1}{2}-x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"82\" height=\"38\" />, since otherwise the solution of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/4/7c4e4f00cdedd3514732792e7d91de572ed35690.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = \\frac{1}{2}-x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"82\" height=\"38\" />, i.e. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/0/9e0e8026b136fd56e6638d539be2fc4a06638d2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = \\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"50\" height=\"38\" />, would be a multiple root. Thus we can pair up the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/f/05f1ffc8a6829f25b0b0366087a70dfd4e06fef6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2000$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" /> distinct roots to obtain <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/7/15744fc3758b186df8c3c318452db523689631fc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2000}{2} = 1000$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"103\" height=\"38\" /> distinct pairs, each of which contains <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> distinct roots that sum to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f960094315d60883495f9b74148e17487ee9584.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />. Summing these pairs will therefore count each root exactly once, as desired, so the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/b/c2b58447ccf0bd308eb3f10c6066341c96c0f3cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1000 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = \\boxed{500}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"127\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>As in Solution 2, observe that if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> is a root, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/7/357b9099c84fd8b6f1c7a3a8f9f7288bdb6cf847.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(\\frac{1}{2}-r\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"72\" height=\"45\" /> is also a root, yielding a pair of roots that sum to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f960094315d60883495f9b74148e17487ee9584.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />. This motivates us to substitute <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/2/e82c5544b51c3beca32159b9f63232b1071b8e71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y = x-\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"99\" height=\"43\" />, i.e. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/d/31dc0b7f0c3dfbc35e6787038d7851e9574e5d46.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y = x-\\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"81\" height=\"38\" />, so that the left-hand side of the equation becomes even more symmetric: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/b/bebe2b19700ce7a2f5b958c41b2239c69bccb901.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\left(\\frac{1}{4}+y\\right)^{2001}+\\left(\\frac{1}{4}-y\\right)^{2001} = 0.\\]\" width=\"252\" height=\"46\" /> Using the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Binomial_Theorem\" title=\"Binomial Theorem\">Binomial Theorem</a>, this expands as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/a/f8a519b1f30d3ef836aa37bd9e535cb80c6143a1.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2\\cdot\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot\\binom{2001}{1}y^{2000}-0y^{1999}+\\dotsb = 0,\\]\" width=\"298\" height=\"53\" /> so by <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Vieta%27s_formulas\" title=\"Vieta&#39;s formulas\">Vieta's formulae</a>, the sum of the roots for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> is 0. Since the original equation in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> has degree <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/f/05f1ffc8a6829f25b0b0366087a70dfd4e06fef6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2000$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" />, there are also <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/f/05f1ffc8a6829f25b0b0366087a70dfd4e06fef6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2000$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" /> roots for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />, all of which are distinct since there are no multiple roots. Therefore, letting these roots be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/5/a756f39048cfdcfe5150c58a4def514fefe26a67.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_n = y_n+\\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"99\" height=\"38\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/d/52dced9fc197bed1f71941c76b03357500d0728c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 \\leq n \\leq 2000$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"107\" height=\"16\" />, their sum is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/1/6b163f7ad5defde18228c4fae121a1281efaa346.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sum_{n=1}^{2000}\\left(y_n+\\frac{1}{4}\\right) = \\sum_{n=1}^{2000}y_n + 2000 \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} = 0 + 500 =\\boxed{500}.\\]\" width=\"414\" height=\"50\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4\">Solution 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We begin by introducing a substitution. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/6/296a9e49375cd24dd2b064f1461d6ea17571661b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=\\frac{1}{4}-y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"81\" height=\"38\" />. Substituting this into our equation gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/9/6a99cb0aa14bc2caedcf761d073200cf35cb4610.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\left(\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)^{2001}-2001\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)^{2000}y+\\binom{2001}{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)^{1999}y^2+\\cdots-y^{2001}\\right)+\\left(\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)^{2001}+2001\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)^{2000}y+\\binom{2001}{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)^{1999}y^2+\\cdots+y^{2001}\\right)=0.\\]\" width=\"1075\" height=\"53\" /> We can expand this out, canceling common terms, giving us, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/2/c82642a6b830d5a74c80ad697267bae82e3c5c44.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)^{2001}+2\\binom{2001}{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)^{1999}y^2+\\cdots+2\\binom{2001}{2000}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)y^{2000}=0.\\]\" width=\"521\" height=\"53\" /> We can clean it up by simply multiplying both sides by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, giving, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/2/f52bce461cc9cec82ce7feb0331a016692f6aede.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{1}{4^{2000}}+\\binom{2001}{2}\\frac{y^2}{4^{1998}}+\\binom{2001}{4}\\frac{y^4}{4^{1996}}+\\cdots+\\binom{2001}{2000}y^{2000}.\\]\" width=\"463\" height=\"53\" /> At first it may seem like we haven't progressed whatsoever, but we can revisit what the problem is asking. We aren't trying to find every root of this, but rather the sum of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c339afa03e060d959e4942be7522c0c992f4c41c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> minus every root. Assume the roots were <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/6/9e63509cd097749713c1e2cb60237dae1ba3a96e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r_1,r_2,...$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"64\" height=\"13\" />. This means that our answer would be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/f/f1f9899c801068bc45b0d8e35d4e4cc07db29537.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{4}n-(r_1+r_2+...)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"153\" height=\"38\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is the number of roots. By Vieta's formulas, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/9/98914b052b743d9019edc5d8f02870bf1fd4a712.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r_1+r_2+...=-\\frac{0}{\\binom{2001}{2000}}=0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -21px\" width=\"211\" height=\"46\" />. Thus all we need to find is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/a/beade99a287333439a03bba79a4adad3d01cd75a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{4}n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"26\" height=\"38\" />, which is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c339afa03e060d959e4942be7522c0c992f4c41c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> of the total number of roots. By the fundamental theorem of algebra, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/f/05f1ffc8a6829f25b0b0366087a70dfd4e06fef6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2000$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" /> roots of this polynomial. This gives us a final answer of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/b/38b3c7fb8269096efff1e571748c2133521de7e1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 2000$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"64\" height=\"38\" />, which is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/3/ee3728678ba7735d770f0faddfa933f469f6a36f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{500}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"39\" height=\"23\" />\n</p><p>~littleduckysteve\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_AIME_I\" title=\"2001 AIME I\">2001 AIME I</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_AIME_I_Problems\" title=\"2001 AIME I Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_AIME_I_Answer_Key\" title=\"2001 AIME I Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=45&amp;year=2001\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2001 AIME I Problems/Problem 2\">Problem 2</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2001 AIME I Problems/Problem 4\">Problem 4</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2001 AIME I Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2001 AIME I Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•<